-Feb-2026<br><br>Dear Mr. Okuyama,<br><br>PSY-2025-0213 entitled &quot;Freq=
uentist forecasting in regime-switching models with extended Hamilton filte=
r&quot;, which you submitted to Psychometrika, has been reviewed. The comme=
nts of the reviewers and associate editor are included at the bottom of thi=
s letter. While they recognise the potential of your manuscript as a valuab=
le contribution to the journal, they also suggest some major revisions to y=
our manuscript. Consequently, I invite you to respond to their comments and=
 submit a revised version of your manuscript. To start the process of submi=
tting the revision, please click the link below:<br><br>*** PLEASE NOTE: Th=
is is a two-step process. After clicking on the link, you will be directed =
to a webpage to confirm. ***<br><br>https://mc.manuscriptcentral.com/psycho=
metrika?URL_MASK=3Dd4a6e6260df94386ae35987aff3b3a2c<br><br>Alternatively, y=
ou may log into your author centre at https://mc.manuscriptcentral.com/psyc=
hometrika, where you will find your manuscript under &quot;Manuscripts Awai=
ting Revision&quot;.<br><br>Upon submission of the revised version, your ma=
nuscript number will be appended to denote a revision. When submitting your=
 revised manuscript, you must respond to the comments made by the reviewer(=
s) in the space provided. Please use this space to document any changes you=
 make to the original manuscript. &nbsp;In order to expedite the processing=
 of the revised manuscript, please be as specific as possible in your respo=
nse to the reviewer(s), and mark the altered text in your revised paper usi=
ng, e.g. a different font colour, or highlighting. Because we are trying to=
 facilitate timely publication of manuscripts submitted to Psychometrika, y=
our revised manuscript should be uploaded as soon as possible. We expect to=
 receive your revision by 09-Jun-2026. If it is not possible for you to sub=
mit your revision by this date, please contact the Editorial Office to rear=
range the due date. Otherwise we may have to consider your paper as a new s=
ubmission.<br><br>Once again, thank you for submitting your manuscript to P=
sychometrika and I look forward to receiving your revision.<br><br>Sincerel=
y,<br>Sandip Sinharay<br>Editor-in-Chief, Psychometrika<br>Linda Cook Chair=
 in the Science of Measurement, ETS Research Institute<br><br>Associate Edi=
tor<br>Comments to the Author:<br>09-Feb-2026<br><br>Dear Mr. Okuyama,<br><=
br>Manuscript ID PSY-2025-0213 entitled &quot;Frequentist forecasting in re=
gime-switching models with extended Hamilton filter&quot;, which you submit=
ted to Psychometrika, has been reviewed.<br><br>Thank you for submitting yo=
ur research to Psychometrika.<br><br>Our referees have now considered your =
paper and have provided excellent feedback. The comments of the reviewer ar=
e included at the bottom of this letter. In addition, I have read the manus=
cript carefully myself. The reviews are in general favorable and suggest th=
at, subject to some major revisions, your paper could be suitable for publi=
cation.<br><br>Please focus especially on the comments regarding simulation=
 studies. Additionally, please ensure that the formatting is correct throug=
hout the manuscript. For example, on p. 9 the footnote callouts should be p=
laced after punctuation marks.<br><br>Once again, thank you for submitting =
your manuscript to Psychometrika and I look forward to receiving your revis=
ion.<br><br>Sincerely,<br>Dr. Hongyun Liu<br>Associate Editor, Psychometrik=
a<br>hyliu@bnu.edu.cn<br><br><br>Reviewer(s)' Comments to Author:<br>Review=
er: 1<br><br>Comments to the Author<br>The manuscript is well written and n=
icely structured. The authors articulate the advantages of a frequentist ap=
proach over a Bayesian one, and the proposed algorithm is presented in an e=
asy-to-follow manner. The real-data illustration and simulation study effec=
tively demonstrate the implementation and predictive performance of the met=
hod, making it accessible to researchers interested in applying it to inten=
sive longitudinal data. Overall, the proposed frequentist approach for esti=
mation and forecasting in regime-switching state-space models is interestin=
g and useful. I have one major comment, followed by several minor points.<b=
r><br><br>Major comment<br>While the authors highlight that the frequentist=
 approach avoids the need to specify priors, it still requires starting val=
ues to initialize the Kim filter. Prior work has shown that maximum<br>like=
lihood estimation in state-space models can be sensitive to initial values =
at time=3D0, with<br>implications for convergence and parameter estimation.=
 It would strengthen the manuscript to include a discussion of this issue. =
Specifically, how sensitive is the proposed method to starting values, and =
what strategies=E2=80=94if any=E2=80=94are recommended for specifying start=
ing values? Should preliminary analyses or prior empirical findings be used=
 to narrow the plausible range of starting values?<br><br>Minor comments<br=
>Page 2, Line 9: It would be helpful to clarify the meaning of =E2=80=9Cnon=
linear dynamics.=E2=80=9D Are the authors referring to nonlinear temporal t=
rajectories, or to nonlinear functional forms (e.g., sigmoid function) betw=
een transition probabilities and their predictors?<br><br>Page 3 Line 10: =
=E2=80=9CVAR=E2=80=9D needs to be spelled out as =E2=80=9Cvector autoregres=
sive=E2=80=9D at its first mention.<br><br>Page 4, Section 2.3 (Between-lev=
el structural model): Several dimensional inconsistencies appear in this se=
ction.<br>(1) Should b2s be a U1 =C3=97 U2 matrix rather than a U1 =C3=97 1=
 vector?<br>(2) Should Q2 be a U1 =C3=97 U1 matrix rather than U2 =C3=97 U2=
?<br>(3) Should B4s be a U1 =C3=97 U2 matrix rather than U1 =C3=97 U1? Even=
 with this correction, B4s=CE=B72i becomes a U1 =C3=97 1 vector and still c=
annot be added to the U1 =C3=97 U1 matrix B3s.<br>The matrix dimensions in =
Equation 2.5 appear to imply that U2 =3D 1, meaning =CE=B72 is treated as a=
 single latent factor. If this is the intended specification, please clarif=
y the rationale for restricting =CE=B72 to one factor. If not, the dimensio=
nal inconsistencies in the equation should be corrected.<br>It is also uncl=
ear why B4s is described as a =E2=80=9Cmatrix of conditional interaction ef=
fects=E2=80=9D; my understanding is that it represents the effect of =CE=B7=
2 on B3is.<br>Equation 2.5 does not include a random-effect term. Is this o=
mission intentional, e.g., to ensure model identification or to simplify th=
e model to improve convergence?<br>Are the VAR(1) parameters in B3is constr=
ained to lie between =E2=80=931 and 1 to ensure stationarity? If so, how ar=
e these constraints imposed on B3s and B4s?<br><br>Page 4 Section 2.4: Rega=
rding Ys=E2=80=992, since this is a scalar coefficient, does this imply tha=
t there is a single inter-individual factor (i.e., =CE=B72 is scalar and U2=
 =3D 1)? If so, this should be stated explicitly, and as I mentioned earlie=
r, the rationale for restricting =CE=B72 to one factor should be clarified.=
<br><br>Page 6 Line 22: The manuscript notes that Paug,0I0 is set to a spec=
ific block matrix =E2=80=9Creflecting the prior variance of the random inte=
rcepts =CE=B62.=E2=80=9D It is unclear how this =E2=80=9Cprior variance=E2=
=80=9D is obtained. Is it informed by previous studies, or estimated from t=
he data? Why do the authors choose not to use a diffuse density with large =
constants in the covariance matrix, as is commonly done? Is this choice mot=
ivated by convergence considerations?<br>Page 11 Line 11: There is a typo. =
